# Makes the "engines" field in package.json a guardrail rather than a warning. Without this,
# npm prints an EBADENGINE notice on an unsupported Node version and installs anyway, which is
# the failure mode this is here to prevent: docs/SETUP.md used to say "check it yourself" and
# nothing enforced it.
#
# Since 2026-09-07 it also enforces `engines.npm`, which is pinned to an EXACT version while
# `engines.node` stays a floor. The asymmetry is deliberate and the two constraints are doing
# different jobs.
#
# Node is pinned loosely because any version at or above the floor works, and pinning it would
# manufacture a problem -- Node 26 is known good, and a hard pin would refuse it.
#
# npm is pinned exactly because the *resolver version itself* is the thing that varies. On
# 2026-09-07 `npm install` in apps/web rewrote 26 lines of package-lock.json with no dependency
# added, removed, or changed -- purely npm's peer-marking bookkeeping, differing between npm
# 11.12.1 locally and npm 11.19.0 on CI. That diff is indistinguishable at a glance from a real
# dependency change, and it lands inside whatever unrelated pull request happened to run an
# install. A floor would not catch it: both versions are above any floor worth setting.
#
# Corepack would have been the better mechanism -- `packageManager` installs the pinned npm
# rather than merely refusing the wrong one. It was checked and rejected on evidence: corepack
# was unbundled from Node in v25, so it is absent on Node 26 (this project's local version) and
# present only on the CI runner's Node 24. A mechanism that exists on one side of the comparison
# cannot make the two sides agree.
engine-strict=true
